Info-ZIP UnZip 4.1 -- authors' terms
====================================

The source in this directory is Info-ZIP UnZip 4.1, taken from
mwcbbs/386/zip/unzip.tar.Z.  Seven of its nine files are byte-identical to that
archive; unzip.c and unzip.h carry this port's changes, which are the Z8001
segmented-pointer and 16-bit int corrections, not changes to the algorithms.


Samuel H. Smith's notice, verbatim
----------------------------------

Reproduced in unzip.c under the heading "Copyright, originally from version
1.2 (?)":

     * Copyright 1989 Samuel H. Smith;  All rights reserved
     *
     * Do not distribute modified versions without my permission.
     * Do not remove or alter this notice or any other copyright notice.
     * If you use this in your own program you must distribute source code.
     * Do not use any of this in a commercial product.

UnZip 4.1 also carries "Copyright 1985 System Enhancement Associates" in the
implode/reduce headers.


How Info-ZIP distributed 4.1
----------------------------

unzip41.des, the release description shipped in the same archive:

    UNZIP41.ZIP     Generic Unix unzip v4.1 for free public distribution
    ...
    A public distribution version of the Info-ZIP project's generic
    Unix unzip utility.
    ...
    Information on commercial usage, licensing, etc. is NOT available.
    We at Info-ZIP are currently researching how to best handle this
    problem.  (There are potential copyright problems.)


Info-ZIP's own record of what Smith intended
--------------------------------------------

Info-ZIP later resolved the question and wrote down both Smith's position and
their reading of it.  From COPYING.OLD in the UnZip distribution (the file is
dated 17 Jul 2000; the enquiry it reports was made in mid-May 1994):

    Regarding the first stipulation, Mr. Smith was tracked down in southern
    California some years back [...]:

    "He says that he thought that whoever contacted him understood that
     he has no objection to the Info-ZIP group's inclusion of his code.
     His primary concern is that it remain freely distributable, he said."

    Despite the fact that our "normal" code has been entirely rewritten
    and by default no longer contains any of Mr. Smith's code, Info-ZIP
    remains indebted and grateful to him.  [...]

    Note that the third and fourth stipulations still apply to any com-
    pany that wishes to incorporate the unreduce code into its products;
    if you wish to do so, you must contact Mr. Smith directly regarding
    licensing.

The same file records where the Smith-derived code ends:

    All remaining code is now (starting with UnZip version 5.41) covered
    by the new Info-ZIP license.
    [...]
    As far as we can tell, only the core code of the unreduce method
    remained substantially similar to Mr. Smith's original source.  As of
    UnZip 5.42, the complete core code is now covered by the Info-ZIP
    Licence.  Therefore, support for the reduce method has been removed.

So the clean version of this program exists and its terms are settled: any
UnZip from 5.41 onward is under the Info-ZIP licence, which permits commercial
use and modification.  4.1 is still what ships because the later sources assume
a 32-bit int and an ANSI compiler, which this target has neither of; the upgrade
is a port of its own rather than a newer tarball.
